BREAK-EVEN
============================================================================================================
  mean round-trip cost per trade      : $33.51  (fees $6.76 + spreads $26.76)
  basket sum must therefore move      :  33.51c  in 2h, in the predicted direction
  it actually moves (mean |delta|)    :   2.26c
  90th percentile |delta|             :   6.00c

  The bar is above the 90th percentile of what the basket sum actually
  does. Fewer than 10% of opportunities could clear costs even with a
  PERFECT forecast, so no improvement in forecast accuracy fixes this.
  The constraint is the round trip: entry and exit on every leg.

============================================================================================================
HOW TO READ THIS
============================================================================================================
  model net/trade $-30.59   always_revert net/trade $-28.07
  The model does NOT beat a one-line rule that uses no model at all.
  Whatever the MSE result says, the tradeable content here is 'basket
  dislocation mean-reverts', which is a property of the market, not a
  finding about the STGAT. Report it that way.

  If NET is negative everywhere while gross is positive, the forecast edge is
  real and smaller than the cost of acting on it. That is a legitimate and
  publishable finding -- it is the same shape as the ladder result, and it is
  the honest answer to 'is this strategy profitable'.

  Compare model against static_arb, not against zero. static_arb crosses each
  leg ONCE because settlement is free; the round trip crosses twice. A forecast
  strategy that loses to holding the static arbitrage has not earned its
  complexity even if its own net is positive.
